 ## Background
-The integration of differentiability into volume rendering emerged from the need to optimize 3D structures from 2D data without explicit 3D supervision [43-differentiable-volume-rendering.md]. Early work focused on analytic gradients for differentiable volume rendering [43-differentiable-volume-rendering.md], while later developments unified tomographic reconstruction with inverse rendering techniques [45-tomographic-reconstruction-as-inverse-rendering.md]. The field has evolved to support both neural implicit representations and explicit grid-based optimizations, driven by applications in novel view synthesis and medical imaging [G].
